WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.480
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.780
In this video I'm going to teach you how you can calculate cube roots (∛) in your head.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.000
Why would you ever want to do this?

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:13.000
The Education Secretary Nicky Morgan was recently on TV and put on the hot seat.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.240
She was asked by a ten-year-old the question,

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.620
"What Is the cube root of 125?"

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.140
She refused to answer any math questions on TV and I understand that.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.580
But if she knew the trick in this video, she could have answered it very quickly and done many more math problems.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.540
So to do the trick, you first have to memorize the cubes of each number from 1 to 10.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.140
The cube of a number is the number multiplied by itself three times.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.840
So the cube of 1 is still 1.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.840
The cube of 2 is 8.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.480
The cube of 3 is 27.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.720
And I'm going to keep writing the cubes of each number.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.240
So you may know some of these.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.940
Otherwise, to do this trick you'll have to memorize these ten numbers.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.180
Now once we've written down these cubes, I want you to focus on the very last digit of the number of the cube.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.120
So what's important is that each of these 10 numbers end in a different last digit.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.140
And that'll be very important to doing this trick.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.720
So this will be another thing you have to memorize.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.500
When you memorize these 10 digits, you should also remember the last digit.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.840
So an easy way to remember this is that for the numbers 1, 4, 5, 6, 9, and 0,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.040
the last digit is the same as the numbers.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.700
And for the numbers 2 and 8, and 3 and 7, the last digit flips.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.740
So the last digit for 2 is 8, and the last digit for 3 is 7 and vice versa.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.240
So let's do the trick.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.520
What's the cube root of 39,304?

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.080
The first thing I want you to do is look at the last digit.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.040
The last digit of 39,304 is 4,

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.340
which we're going to put in a box.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.360
So we look up 4 in the last digit,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.340
and that corresponds to the number 4.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.640
So we write down the number 4.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.860
The next step is we ignore the last three digits of our number.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:16.960
So in 39,304, we're going to just ignore the 304.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.980
And now we want to find the closest cube without going over.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.200
So 39 is between 27 and 64,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.760
so it's actually closest to 27 without going over,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.260
which means it's closest to the cube of 3.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.340
And that gives us the other part of our answer, which is 3.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.860
So here we have it, we put the numbers together and 34 Is the cube root of 39,304.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.020
So why would this ever be useful?

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.800
So here's a trick you can do.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.560
Ask someone to pick a number from 1 to 100,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.560
and just think about it secretly, don't have them tell you the number.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.260
Have them cube the number on a calculator.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.180
And once they cube the number, they tell you the cube

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.700
and you instantly can tell them the cube root

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.060
by using this table and using the trick.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:16.440
So let's imagine they tell you the number is 636,056.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.920
So we look at the last digit, which is 6,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.940
and when the last digit is 6, that corresponds to the number 6.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:29.680
And then we look at the, we ignore the last three digits and we look at 636.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.200
That's between the cubes of 8 and 9, so we put 8.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.300
And therefore, the cube root is 86.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.320
So once you get used, once you've memorized this table, you'll actually be able to do this very quickly.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.660
Let's do another example.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.080
185,193

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.800
We look at the last digit of 3.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.420
That corresponds to the number 7 because 3 and 7 flip.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.040
Then we ignore the last three digits and we look at 185.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.500
And that's closest to the cube of 5 without going over.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.940
So that gives us the answer of 57.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.320
So it's a pretty neat trick.

